The first thing any program in this project does is read this directory.

Right now there is nothing to configure, because nothing has been built. When
there is, this is where a session's opening decisions live: the ones made before
anybody connects, which should be readable, diffable, and copyable rather than
buried in a wall of command-line flags.

The reason this is a directory of files: a session is a small number of
decisions, and the header of a replay is nearly the same set of fields. Anything
you would want to pin down and hand to somebody else so they can run the same
session belongs here.

What goes here eventually:

    seed              -- the session seed, one integer. Everything random in the
                         world and in the ruleset descends from it through named
                         streams.
    world             -- which world file to load, or the description and seed to
                         generate one from. See docs/013-content-is-generated.md.
    ruleset           -- which directory of Lua to load as the rules layer.
                         See docs/011-the-rules-layer.md.
    door              -- the port that is always open, and the range of private
                         ports it hands out. See docs/003-the-door-and-the-private-port.md.
    participants      -- one entry per person: who they are, what proves it, and
                         which scopes they hold. Permission is looked up here and
                         never claimed by a client.
    tick              -- how many beats per second the world advances.
    fog               -- how coarse the memory grid is.

Two of those -- the tick rate and the fog cell size -- are open questions rather
than settled defaults. See docs/016-open-questions.md, 3.2 and 2.1.
